ftware Engineering (ASE), pp. 1186–1189. Cited by: cite99†§2.2 .\nL463:   * [25] B. Mueller File 1 of. Cited by: cite97†§2.2 .\nL464:   * Nath et al. (2014) K. Nath, S. Dhar, and S. Basishtha Web 1.0 to web 3.0-evolution of the web and its various challenges. In 2014 International Conference on Reliability Optimization and Information Technology (ICROIT), pp. 86–89. Cited by: cite108†§1 .\nL465:   * [27] () Not-so-smart-contracts: Examples of Solidity security issues. Note: cite110†https://github.com/crytic/not-so-smart-contracts†github.com [Accessed 09-01-2025] Cited by: cite111†§3.2.1 .\nL466:   * Park et al. (2024) J. Park, T. Zhang, W. Grieskamp, M. Xu, G. Di Giacomo, K. Chen, Y. Lu, and R. Chen Securing aptos framework with formal verification. In 5th International Workshop on Formal Methods for Blockchains (FMBC 2024), Cited by: cite109†§1 .\nL467:   * Popper (2016) N. Popper A hacking of more than $50 million dashes hopes in the world of virtual currency. The New York Times 17. Cited by: cite95†§1 .\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTowards a Wireless Physical-Layer Foundation Model: Challenges and Strategies (https://arxiv.org/html/2403.12065v2)\nciteturn6698view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn6697view2\",\"pattern\":\"## III\"}); Total lines: 270\nL128: defined channel selection and power allocation parameters for each application); they will be able to simply instruct (prompt) cite25†LLM (Towards a Wireless Physical-Layer Foundation Model: Challenges and Strategies) to configure networks or build applications using in-context learning (giving input and output examples) and utilize automatic optimization (without explicit programming) with physical-layer sensing cite26†WPFM (Towards a Wireless Physical-Layer Foundation Model: Challenges and Strategies) , as illustrated in Figure 1.\nL129: ## III Challenges in wireless physical-layer foundation models\nL130: Recent scientific work has provided preliminary building blocks for cite26†WPFM (Towards a Wireless Physical-Layer Foundation Model: Challenges and Strategies) , such as transfer learning solutions and per-task data representations. However, since there is no general methodology to integrate these building blocks into an overall foundation model, significant research remains to be done.\nL131: In addition, the usage of cite25†LLM (Towards a Wireless Physical-Layer Foundation Model: Challenges and Strategies) has been a recent research topic and even standardization within wireless communications, however, no physical-layer results have been presented and fundamental research challenges have yet to be identified [cite51†19 , cite52†20 , cite49†17 , cite50†18 ]. This paper addresses the following fundamental research challenges:\nL132: #### III-1 Challenge 1: Novel methods are required for wireless pre-training tasks\nL133: Modern ML architectures are capable of handling complex pattern learning and scaling of many parameters. The transformer neural network [cite53†21 ], for example, excels in efficiently learning sequential data with parallel training.\nL134: In other domains, foundation models exist which can perform general tasks, such as CLIP, DALL-E and cite25†LLM (Towards a Wireless Physical-Layer Foundation Model: Challenges and Strategies) such as GPT3, GPT4 and BERT, but also have been applied recently in time series [cite54†22 , cite55†23 ]. However, in wireless networks, the transformer architecture is missing its potential by being trained to perform individual tasks.\nL135: Instead, training large foundation transformer models requires effective un- and self-supervised pre-training tasks, e.g., in cite25†LLM (Towards a Wireless Physical-Layer Foundation Model: Challenges and Strategies) next-word predictions, masking, positional predictions, denoising, etc., are used [cite40†9 ]. This is key in enabling a shared foundation model for multiple downstream tasks (Figure 1).\nL136: However, with limited data availability in the past and challenges in supporting heterogeneous data types, there is a gap in the wireless domain for effective pre-training tasks.\nL137: #### III-2 Challenge 2: cite26†WPFM (Towards a Wireless Physical-Layer Foundation Model: Challenges and Strategies) need to support the embedding of heterogeneous wireless time series with different lengths, sampling rates and data types\nL138: Creating a foundation model for wireless time series, such as cite56†IQ (cite56†IQ (Towards a Wireless Physical-Layer Foundation Model: Challenges and Strategies) ) samples and cite57†CIR , diverges significantly from models in the NLP (i.e. semantic understanding) and vision domains (i.e. 2D / 3D patterns) due to the heterogeneous temporal nature of the data.\nL242:   * [17] L. Bariah, Q. Zhao, H. Zou, Y. Tian, F. Bader, and M. Debbah (2023) Large language models for telecom: the next big thing?. arXiv preprint arXiv:2306.10249. Cited by: cite76†§II-B , cite85†§III .\nL243:   * [18] W. Tong, C. Peng, T. Yang, F. Wang, J. Deng, R. Li, L. Yang, H. Zhang, D. Wang, M. Ai, et al. (2023) Ten issues of netgpt. arXiv preprint arXiv:2311.13106. Cited by: cite76†§II-B , cite85†§III .\nL244:   * [19] Z. Lin, G. Qu, Q. Chen, X. Chen, Z. Chen, and K. Huang (2023) Pushing large language models to the 6g edge: vision, challenges, and opportunities. arXiv preprint arXiv:2309.16739. Cited by: cite76†§II-B , cite85†§III .\nL245:   * [20] A. Maatouk, N. Piovesan, F. Ayed, A. De Domenico, and M. Debbah (2023) Large language models for telecom: forthcoming impact on the industry. arXiv preprint arXiv:2308.06013. Cited by: cite76†§II-B , cite85†§III .\nL246:   * [21] A. Vaswani, N. Shazeer, N. Parmar, J. Uszkoreit, L. Jones, A. N. Gomez, Ł. Kaiser, and I. Polosukhin (2017) Attention is all you need. Advances in neural information processing systems 30. Cited by: cite77†§III-1 .\nL247:   * [22] Y. Wang, Z. Gao, D. Zheng, S. Chen, D. Gunduz, and H. V. Poor (2022) Transformer-empowered 6g intelligent networks: from massive mimo processing to semantic communication. IEEE Wireless Communications. Cited by: cite77†§III-1 .\nL248:   * [23] P. Wang, Y. Cheng, B. Dong, R. Hu, and S. Li (2022) WIR-transformer: using transformers for wireless interference recognition. IEEE Wireless Communications Letters 11 (12), pp. 2472–2476. Cited by: cite77†§III-1 .\nL249:   * [24] L. H. Li, M. Yatskar, D. Yin, C. Hsieh, and K. Chang (2019) Visualbert: a simple and performant baseline for vision and language. arXiv preprint arXiv:1908.03557. Cited by: cite80†§III-3 .\nL250:   * [25] M. Kotaru (2023) Adapting foundation models for information synthesis of wireless communication specifications. arXiv preprint arXiv:2308.04033. Cited by: cite80†§III-3 .\nL251:   * [26] Z. Zhou, R. Zhong, C. Yang, Y. Wang, X. Yang, and W. Shen (2022) A k-variate time series is worth k words: evolution of the vanilla transformer architecture for long-term multivariate time series forecasting. arXiv preprint arXiv:2212.02789. Cited by: cite86†§V-A .\n"
